              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"vayathu\" — age",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"vayathu\" — age]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "CONNECT",
              "instruction": "Sanskrit vayas, Tamil vayathu, Latin vīs",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU CONNECT: Sanskrit *vayas* · Tamil *vayathu* · Latin *vīs*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Is Tamil's வயது the same Sanskrit word borrowed by all four Dravidian languages here? (Yes — none built a native alternative for this concept.) What did vayas originally include besides age? (Vigor.) Next: choose between Tamil's casual possessive and formal dative-plus-become shapes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "TA-C19-age-register-grammar",
      "sequence": 420,
      "title": "Your age what? / To you how many years becomes?",
      "headword": "உன் வயசு என்ன? / உனக்கு எத்தனை வயது ஆகிறது?",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "casual Tamil uses a possessive age question while formal Tamil uses dative plus become",
      "script": "tamil",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:15d09bbc0d429868",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil did not choose one age construction. Register selects between two shapes already visible elsewhere in the family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: Casual possessive",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "உன் வயசு என்ன? — \"your age what?\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Casual speech uses a plain possessive with no dative and no verb, like Kannada and Telugu."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: Formal dative plus \"become\"",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "உனக்கு எத்தனை வயது ஆகிறது? — \"to you how many years is becoming?\" எனக்கு இருபது வயது ஆகிறது. — \"to me twenty years is becoming.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Formal/written Tamil uses the dative experiencer plus ஆகிறது (aakirathu, \"is becoming,\" from aaku). Malayalam uses a similar dative shape but chooses \"exists\" instead."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Do not compress these into \"Tamil's one grammar.\" Both live in the language; setting and register choose between them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"un vayasu enna?\" — casual",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"un vayasu enna?\" — casual]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"unakku ettanai vayathu aakirathu?\" — formal",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"unakku ettanai vayathu aakirathu?\" — formal]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "ANSWER",
              "instruction": "\"enakku irupathu vayathu aakirathu\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU ANSWER: \"enakku irupathu vayathu aakirathu\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which shape is casual? (Possessive: \"your age what?\") Which is formal? (Dative + aakirathu, \"become.\") Does Malayalam use the same verb? (No — it uses \"exists.\")"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
